{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7EPMH7g35LqZmdegL6j","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before I draft this, I have a few questions. Your answers will change what the letter says.\n\n1. **What's behind \"cut new-hire training time in half\"?** Do you know how long their onboarding takes now, or have you shortened a training program yourself before? As written, it's a promise about a plant you haven't seen. A hiring manager in a regulated industry may read it as not understanding why GMP training takes as long as it does. If you have something concrete behind it, I'll build the claim around that. If not, I'd suggest offering to make new hires competent faster and more consistently, backed by your lab-safety record and your onboarding of new teachers. Which do you want?\n2. **What's the company, and who should the letter go to?** Also tell me the job title exactly as posted, and paste the main requirements if you can. Then I can match their wording and pick which of your results to lead with.\n3. **Have you done anything to close the GMP gap?** For example, a GMP or FDA 21 CFR Part 11 course, reading their LMS vendor's documentation, or talking to someone at the plant. Even one step lets the letter take on that gap directly instead of leaving it as a question.\n4. **Why this move, and why now?** One honest sentence about why you're leaving teaching for manufacturing training helps the reader trust the rest.\n5. **What did the lab safety program actually involve?** For example, written procedures, sign-offs, refresher schedules, records of who was trained on what. If you kept documentation like that, it's the closest thing you have to GMP training records, and I'd put it front and center."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":249,"cache_read_input_tokens":9540,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_t